WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.261
Welcome to Headstart algebra.

2
00:00:02.281 --> 00:00:03.422
This is Lesson 2:

3
00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:05.335
multiplying algebraic terms.

4
00:00:05.355 --> 00:00:10.117
By the end of this video, you'll be able to write terms in index form,

5
00:00:10.137 --> 00:00:12.781
and multiply terms using the index law.

6
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:15.954
First, index form.

7
00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:21.615
When the same letter is multiplied by itself, we can write it with an index,

8
00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:23.105
also called a power.

9
00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:24.520
a³ means a × a × a.

10
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.773
The small raised number, 3, is the index.

11
00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:32.464
It tells us how many times the base is multiplied.

12
00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:34.383
The base is the letter, a.

13
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.988
And a letter with no index has an index of one.

14
00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:40.639
So a is the same as a¹.

15
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:45.535
To write in index form, count each letter.

16
00:00:45.555 --> 00:00:50.781
In 3 × a × a × a × b, the letter a appears three times, and b once.

17
00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:52.112
So we write 3a³b.

18
00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:56.966
The number goes first, then the letters in alphabetical order.

19
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.661
Try another.

20
00:00:59.681 --> 00:01:05.587
2 × x × x × y × y × y has two x's and three y's.

21
00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:07.438
So it is 2x²y³.

22
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:11.962
Before you multiply anything, check the sign.

23
00:01:11.982 --> 00:01:14.553
A positive times a positive is positive.

24
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.494
A negative times a negative is also positive.

25
00:01:18.514 --> 00:01:20.303
Two negatives make a positive.

26
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:23.694
A positive times a negative is negative.

27
00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:28.053
And a negative times a positive is negative too.

28
00:01:28.073 --> 00:01:28.273
So:

29
00:01:28.269 --> 00:01:32.436
same signs give a positive, and different signs give a negative.

30
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.579
Now let's multiply terms.

31
00:01:36.599 --> 00:01:38.910
We'll use this example:

32
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.328
3a³b × −2a²b².

33
00:01:40.348 --> 00:01:42.557
There are three steps.

34
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.357
Step one:

35
00:01:44.377 --> 00:01:46.473
write each term in expanded form.

36
00:01:46.493 --> 00:01:49.742
Every letter is written out in full, one at a time.

37
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:51.605
Step two:

38
00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:56.285
group the coefficients together, and group the matching letters.

39
00:01:56.305 --> 00:01:59.649
That's 3 and −2, then five a's, and three b's.

40
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.220
Step three:

41
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.630
write it back in index form.

42
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:11.721
3 × −2 is −6, five a's is a⁵, and three b's is b³.

43
00:02:11.741 --> 00:02:14.644
So the answer is −6a⁵b³.

44
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:19.946
Writing everything out takes a long time, so here is a shortcut.

45
00:02:19.966 --> 00:02:23.818
Multiply the coefficients, and add the indices of matching letters.

46
00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:34.126
For example, a³ × a⁴ is a × a × a times a × a × a × a, which is seven a's.

47
00:02:34.146 --> 00:02:35.352
That's a⁷.

48
00:02:35.372 --> 00:02:38.788
We simply added the indices:

49
00:02:38.808 --> 00:02:40.015
3 + 4 = 7.

50
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:46.232
Letters that appear in only one term are just copied across.

51
00:02:46.252 --> 00:02:47.894
So 3ab × 2c is 6abc.

52
00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:51.730
Be careful about what an index applies to.

53
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.423
An index only applies to the number or letter immediately to its left.

54
00:02:56.667 --> 00:03:00.725
In 4gh³, the 3 only applies to h.

55
00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:03.938
So it's 4 × g × h × h × h.

56
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:12.751
But in (4gh)³, the brackets make the 3 apply to everything inside.

57
00:03:12.771 --> 00:03:14.825
So 4 is cubed too:

58
00:03:14.845 --> 00:03:17.936
4³ is 64, so we get 64g³h³.

59
00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:24.941
And for a power of a power, multiply the indices.

60
00:03:24.961 --> 00:03:26.360
(x²)³ is x⁶.

61
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.145
Three helpful hints.

62
00:03:29.165 --> 00:03:33.224
First, use brackets when an index should apply to more than one thing.

63
00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:40.679
Second, always write the coefficient first, then the letters in alphabetical order.

64
00:03:40.699 --> 00:03:42.557
So b × 3 × a becomes 3ab.

65
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.414
And third, always check the sign first.

66
00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.441
Two negatives make a positive.

67
00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:55.368
You should now be able to write repeated multiplication in index form,

68
00:03:55.388 --> 00:03:57.725
work out the sign of a product,

69
00:03:57.745 --> 00:04:02.438
multiply terms by multiplying coefficients and adding indices,

70
00:04:02.458 --> 00:04:06.087
and tell the difference between 4gh³ and (4gh)³.

71
00:04:06.107 --> 00:04:12.017
Next, watch the worked examples video, then try the questions in your booklet.

